# Copy to .env for a local run. Nothing here is a secret; the real values live in
# Modal Secrets (see modal_app.py).

# --- The kill switch (§3) ---------------------------------------------------
# Set to 0 to disable NEW analysis jobs and answer a clean "feature unavailable".
# Cached maps keep serving, because a cache hit costs nothing and never touches a
# recording. Read per request — flipping it needs no deploy.
CHORDS_ANALYSIS_ENABLED=1

# --- Auth (same Firebase project as Mo, §16.2) ------------------------------
# Locally, leave the Firebase keys unset and use the dev token instead.
CHORDS_DEV_TOKEN=dev-token
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=      # the key's JSON *content*, or base64 of it
# CHORDS_REQUIRE_AUTH=1               # production: crash rather than 401 everyone.
                                      # Refuses to start if CHORDS_DEV_TOKEN is set.

# --- Storage ----------------------------------------------------------------
CHORDS_DB_PATH=chords.sqlite3
# CHORDS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://…  # set ⇒ Postgres; unset ⇒ SQLite above

# --- Quota (§16.4) ----------------------------------------------------------
# This service's OWN daily quota — an analysis and a Mo song are different
# spends. Cache hits never count against it.
CHORDS_DAILY_QUOTA=10

# --- Abuse hardening --------------------------------------------------------
# 0 disables a scope, which is the default so a local run is unaffected.
CHORDS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=0
CHORDS_RATE_LIMIT_IP_PER_MIN=0
# How many proxy hops in front of this service are OURS — i.e. how far from the
# right end of `X-Forwarded-For` the real client address is. 1 is Modal's own
# proxy, which is this deployment. Raise it if you put something in front:
# behind Cloudflare → load balancer, the rightmost entry is the load balancer's
# address, identical for every caller, so the whole internet collapses into one
# rate-limit bucket and one noisy client throttles every player at once. Too
# high and a caller can forge their own bucket. See `app/main.client_ip`.
CHORDS_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# --- Admin / takedown (§3) --------------------------------------------------
# Unset ⇒ the admin routes answer 503 and /healthz says "unconfigured".
CHORDS_ADMIN_TOKEN=local-admin-secret

# --- Analysis ---------------------------------------------------------------
CHORDS_MAX_VIDEO_SECONDS=600          # §18: songs, not DJ sets
CHORDS_CONFIDENCE_FLOOR=0.5
# --- The time budget, which has to add up -----------------------------------
# These five describe one job's wall clock and they are checked against each
# other by `tests/test_deployment.py`, not just documented:
#
#     probe + fetch + decode + dsp_reserve  ≤  CHORDS_JOB_DEADLINE_S
#                                           <  modal_app.WORKER_TIMEOUT_S (600)
#                                           <  store.Store._JOB_LEASE_S (900)
#
# Read outward: the stages fit inside the deadline, so the deadline is what fails
# a slow job — with a terminal status, a message and a refund. The container
# timeout is the backstop for what the deadline cannot see (a hang inside pure
# compute), and the lease is the backstop for a container that died writing
# nothing. Breaking the chain is how a *successful but slow* fetch used to get
# SIGKILLed with no status written, leaving the player on a spinner for 15
# minutes: the ceilings summed to 405 s, the container died at 300, and the
# deadline claimed to be 180.
CHORDS_JOB_DEADLINE_S=450
CHORDS_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S=45             # metadata lookup — the gate depends on it
CHORDS_FETCH_TIMEOUT_S=120            # audio-only download
CHORDS_DECODE_TIMEOUT_S=90            # ffmpeg to mono 22.05 kHz
CHORDS_DSP_RESERVE_S=180              # headroom for two models + the theory layer
# §15's section *names*, which need a loudness envelope to find. Off means every
# section is `Part N`; the chart, the bars and the sidecar are identical either
# way. (This was read through a `getattr` default against a field that did not
# exist, so it was a permanent no-op until the field was declared.)
CHORDS_STRUCTURE_PROBE=on
# §20.4 — let a song's repeated sections vote their engine mistakes out. One of
# the two parts of the theory layer that edit chords the engine actually
# reported, and neither `lint` nor `lint_sync` can see a bad edit (both check the
# song against itself). It is a flag rather than a constant so it can be turned
# off in production without a deploy. Measured on the real corpus it is worth
# about +0.003 delivered accuracy — real, but within noise on nine tracks.
CHORDS_THEORY_CONSENSUS=on
# §20.8 — the other one, and the one that speaks where the vote cannot: a brief,
# doubtful reading of a root that the rest of the song contradicts is corrected
# onto the quality the song actually plays there. Same root always. Worth another
# +0.003 delivered on the corpus with no track regressing, and 9.8% of injected
# noise removed against 1.1% of correct chords damaged
# (`bench/run_bench.py --noise`).
CHORDS_THEORY_VOCABULARY=on
# §20.9 — the third, and the one that answers "the engine adds variants and the
# song ends up with more chords than it has". That complaint is a *tie*: the
# engine hears the seventh in half the passes of a section, so no majority ever
# forms and §20.4 files every one of those slots as contested while §20.8's mass
# ratio is nowhere near lopsided enough to speak. Where the count cannot decide,
# this settles the slot by which reading the engine actually *believed* — and
# only where the passes differ in colour alone, never in a root or a barline.
# `bench/run_bench.py --noise`: 15.5% of injected noise removed against 1.3% of
# correct chords damaged, up from 14.0%/1.2% without it.
CHORDS_THEORY_BELIEF=on
# §21 — the chart states the song's *form*: every occurrence of a repeated section
# plays that section's own progression, and in a song measured to change chord a
# bar at a time, a change the engine put a beat early is pulled onto the barline.
#
# The odd one out among these four, and worth knowing which way. The three above
# are **corrections** — each asks whether the engine misheard something and is
# gated so that on perfect input it provably changes nothing. This one makes no
# claim that anything was misheard: it says a song does not play its verse eleven
# subtly different ways, and prints one chart for the section. So it *can* flatten
# an occurrence that genuinely differs, and `tests/test_alignment.py` pins the cost
# at one bar in sixteen on the shape of input where that happens.
#
# What it buys: with it off, Creep compiles as 88 distinct bars in one section with
# a ten-chord vocabulary; with it on, as one eight-bar section played eleven times
# with four chords. `repeats` never fired on a real recording before it existed.
# On the ten-song chart corpus it is worth **+0.049 root and +0.058 triad**, nine
# of ten songs improving — much the largest single win in the theory layer, and
# roughly half of it from pulling anticipated chord changes onto the barline
# rather than from the vote.
CHORDS_THEORY_FORM=on
# MUST be a tmpfs / ephemeral mount. app/analysis/scratch.py refuses anything
# under $HOME, inside the working tree, or on an unrecognised filesystem.
CHORDS_SCRATCH_ROOT=/tmp/chords-scratch
# CHORDS_SCRATCH_ALLOW_PREFIX=        # ':'-separated extra prefixes, if you are
                                      # certain they are ephemeral

# --- Engines (§8 step 2 — chosen; see the benchmark table in README) --------
# These are the defaults in code; set them only to override.
#   chords: btc | chroma        beats: beat_this | librosa
# CHORDS_CHORD_ENGINE=btc
# CHORDS_BEAT_TRACKER=beat_this
#
# And which signal §14 reads strumming off (`onsets: harmonic | librosa`).
# `harmonic` runs the onset envelope over the harmonic component, so the drum kit
# stops voting on what the guitarist played; `librosa` reads the full mix, which
# is right for a solo recording and is what turns a groove into straight eighths
# on anything else. `bench/run_bench.py --strum` is where that comes from: on the
# kit specimens the full-mix detector emits every eighth, including a beat the
# guitar never touches. `harmonic` costs ~11s more on a four-minute track.
# CHORDS_ONSET_DETECTOR=harmonic
#
# BTC is a git checkout, not a pip package (no PyPI release). The worker image
# clones it; locally, point this at your own clone of
# https://github.com/jayg996/BTC-ISMIR19 — it ships its own weights.
# CHORDS_BTC_ROOT=./vendor/BTC-ISMIR19
#
# The expected sha256 of `test/btc_model_large_voca.pt`. Pinned in code
# (`adapters/btc.BTC_CHECKPOINT_SHA256`) and only overridden here if you are
# deliberately running different weights. The commit pin says *which* checkpoint;
# the hash says the bytes arrived intact — which matters because the loader has a
# `weights_only=False` fallback, and that flag means "run whatever code this
# pickle asks for". Unrecognised bytes get the safe loader and nothing else.
# CHORDS_BTC_CHECKPOINT_SHA256=

# --- Fetch ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which yt-dlp player client resolves the media URL. Empty restores yt-dlp's own
# default, which on 2026-08-17 returned HTTP 403 on the bytes for **every** video
# tried — probe served, formats listed, download refused, from the same address
# that had just resolved them. Every client offering the audio-only `140` stream
# was refused; `android` was served, and offers only progressive `18`, so a fetch
# now carries ~11 MB of video that ffmpeg throws away. The table is in
# `ytdlp_source._PLAYER_CLIENT`. This knob exists because the setting moves:
# YouTube retires clients and yt-dlp adds them, and no unit test can see it —
# `scripts/real_song_check.py` is the only gate that reaches YouTube at all.
CHORDS_YTDLP_PLAYER_CLIENT=android

# YouTube answers datacentre IPs with a bot check — the most likely failure in
# production, and the one `ytdlp_source` logs at ERROR level so you can tell it
# from a video simply being private.
#
# **Cookies do not fix it.** That was measured, not assumed: across ten
# containers on ten distinct Modal egress IPs, three ordinary uploads resolved on
# two of them — 20%, and identical with and without cookies (6/30 in both arms).
# The check is per **IP reputation**, not per account. Retrying on a fresh
# container is worth more than any credential, because each one is a new IP; the
# real lever is a residential or rotating proxy.
#
# The settings below are therefore left unset here and in the deployed secret.
# They remain supported for age-restricted or members-only video, where a
# signed-in session genuinely is the mechanism:
#
#   ...COOKIES         a path. Local runs, or anywhere you can mount a file.
#   ...COOKIES_CONTENT the file's *contents*. This is the one Modal needs — it
#                      delivers secrets as env vars, and the worker mounts no
#                      Volume, so there is no file for a path to point at.
#
# Export a Netscape cookies.txt from a browser signed in to YouTube. The path
# wins if both are set. Treat either as a live credential for that account.
# CHORDS_YTDLP_COOKIES=/run/secrets/youtube-cookies.txt
# CHORDS_YTDLP_COOKIES_CONTENT=
# CHORDS_FFMPEG=ffmpeg
#
# **This is the setting that fixes the bot check.** A rotating residential pool,
# as `scheme://user:pass@host:port`. Both adjectives matter:
#
#   rotating     one address per request. A pool that pins you to a single exit
#                gets that exit flagged and then fails forever.
#   residential  the check is on address *reputation*. A static datacentre IP —
#                which is what a cloud provider's static-egress feature sells,
#                Modal's `modal.Proxy` included — is the fingerprint it looks
#                for, and is strictly worse than the unproxied default: it
#                trades a 1-in-6 draw for a permanent no with no retry into.
#
# The cost is bounded by the cache, which is what makes it affordable: a chord
# map is stored per videoId and shared across every user, so a proxied fetch is
# paid once per *song ever*, not once per play. At `-f worstaudio[abr>=64]` a
# four-minute song is ~2-4 MB, so at typical 2026 pricing ($2-8/GB) that is
# roughly a cent the first time anyone analyses a song and nothing after.
#
# Setting this also shrinks the worker's retry budget from twelve containers to
# three (`modal_app.egress_attempt_budget`) — unproxied the retry *is* the
# mitigation; proxied it is only insurance.
# CHORDS_YTDLP_PROXY=

# --- CORS -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty ⇒ no CORS middleware at all. The iOS app isn't a browser.
CHORDS_CORS_ORIGINS=
